# Part Two — Today's Debates

> Topic 7 questions ask you to **judge** and **devise**: which strategies are workable, whether laws work, what masculinity studies adds. Each debate below gives you the facts, both sides and a line to take.

## Debate 1 · More laws, or better enforcement?

Pakistan has passed many laws since ==2006==: harassment (2010), acid and anti-women practices (2011), provincial domestic-violence laws (2013–2025), honour killing and rape (2016), the Anti-Rape Act (2021). Yet conviction rates in 2024 were about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing (SSDO).
- **For more laws:** gaps remain — marital rape is not clearly recognised; forced conversion is not a specific crime nationally; child marriage at 16 survives in most provinces; honour killings can still be compounded if re-labelled.
- **For enforcement:** the main problem is police refusal, poor investigation, slow courts, pressured settlements and lack of shelters.
- **Line to take:** close a few **specific gaps**, but put most effort into **enforcement** — FIR registration, forensic capacity, special courts, witness protection and services in every district.

## Debate 2 · Mediation and reconciliation — help or harm?

The Punjab Act 2016 allows Protection Centres and committees to try **mediation and reconciliation**.
- **For:** many women want the violence to stop, not the marriage to end; mediation is quick, cheap and culturally familiar.
- **Against:** in an unequal relationship, "reconciliation" can pressure women back into danger; families use it to avoid prosecution.
- **Line to take:** mediation only with the woman's **free consent**, never for serious violence, and with safety checks and follow-up.

## Debate 3 · Is the family-forgiveness loophole really closed?

The ==2016== law requires life imprisonment for honour killings even after forgiveness.
- **Optimists:** more prosecutions, greater awareness, a clear signal from Parliament.
- **Sceptics:** courts must first find that the killing was "for honour"; families claim other motives; the **Qandeel Baloch** acquittal (==2022==) showed that pardons still free killers.
- **Line to take:** make all GBV-related murders **non-compoundable** and train courts to recognise honour motives.

## Debate 4 · The GPS bracelet and "Western" law

The Punjab Act's **GPS tracker** provision and its recognition of psychological and economic abuse drew charges from the Council of Islamic Ideology and religious parties that the law was "un-Islamic" and would destroy families.
- **Defenders:** Islam forbids cruelty and injustice; protecting women from violence protects families.
- **Line to take:** frame protection laws in **Islamic and constitutional** terms (Articles 9, 14, 25, 35) and work with scholars — opposition weakens when laws are seen as protecting families.

## Debate 5 · Engaging men — or centring women?

Masculinity studies and programmes such as **Rozan**'s urge work with men and boys.
- **For:** men commit most violence; changing masculinity is the only way to prevent it.
- **Against:** money and attention may shift away from women survivors.
- **Line to take:** do both — **protect women now** and **change men for the future**.

## Debate 6 · Media and the "image" of Pakistan

Mukhtaran Mai, Malala and Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy were all accused of "defaming" Pakistan abroad.
- **Critics:** foreign attention to Pakistan's worst cases distorts its image and serves outside agendas.
- **Defenders:** silence protects abusers; global attention forced reforms (2006, 2011, 2016).
- **Line to take:** the problem is the **violence**, not its exposure; Pakistan's image is improved by **justice**, not silence.

## Debate 7 · Social media — new danger or new tool?

- **Danger:** cyber-harassment, blackmail, and "honour" violence against women who post online (Qandeel Baloch 2016; Sana Yousaf 2025).
- **Tool:** viral videos forced action in the **Degari** killings (Balochistan, ==2025==); online campaigns and helplines support survivors.
- **Line to take:** strengthen **PECA** enforcement against abusers while protecting women's online freedom.
